WEBVTT

00:01:28.579 --> 00:01:30.621
his bad behavior at the game

00:01:30.663 --> 00:01:34.038
might outweigh his good
behavior at the park.

00:01:34.079 --> 00:01:36.663
Research suggests that this bias occurs

00:01:36.704 --> 00:01:40.121
because immoral behaviors
are more diagnostic, or revealing,

00:01:40.163 --> 00:01:42.538
of a person’s true character.

00:01:42.704 --> 00:01:45.121
Okay, so by this logic,

00:01:45.163 --> 00:01:48.829
bad is always stronger than good
when it comes to updating.

00:01:48.871 --> 00:01:50.163
Well, not necessarily.

00:01:50.204 --> 00:01:52.746
Certain types of learning
don’t seem to lead

00:01:52.788 --> 00:01:54.788
to this sort of negativity bias.

00:01:54.829 --> 00:01:55.829
When learning about

00:01:55.871 --> 00:01:58.621
another person’s abilities
and competencies, for instance,

00:01:58.663 --> 00:02:00.246
this bias flips.